.TH tar "" "Command"
.SH NAME
tar \- tape archive manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtar [crtux][0\-7bflmvwzU] [\fIblocks\fB] [\fIarchive\fB] \fIfile ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
tar -- tape archive manager
Usage: tar [crtux][0-7bflmvwU] [blocks] [archive] file ...
.HE
.B tar
saves and restores files in a machine-independent archive format;
its name is short for
.IR "tape archive" ,
though the archive may equally well be an ordinary file or a floppy
disk instead of a magnetic tape.
.PP
The first argument to
.B tar
is a string of option letters.
Exactly one of
.BR c ,
.BR r ,
.BR t ,
.BR u ,
or
.B x
must appear, selecting the function to perform; the remaining letters
are modifiers.
Any following arguments are
.I file
operands, added to, extracted from, listed against, or updated in the
archive; if none are given,
.B c
and
.B r
operate on the current directory (recursively), and
.B t
and
.B x
operate on every file already in the archive.
.IP \fBc\fR
Create a new archive from the named
.I file
operands, including, recursively, the contents of any that are
directories.
.IP \fBr\fR
Append the named
.I file
operands to the end of an existing archive.
.IP \fBt\fR
List the name of each
.I file
in the archive that matches an operand, or every file if none are
given.
With
.BR v ,
also list the mode, group, owner, size, and modification date of each
entry.
.IP \fBu\fR
Like
.BR r ,
but append a
.I file
only if it is not already in the archive or is newer than the copy
already there.
.IP \fBx\fR
Extract the named
.I file
operands from the archive, or every file if none are given, restoring
each one's mode, owner, and group and, unless
.B m
is given, its modification time.
.PP
The following modifiers may be combined with the function letter:
.IP \fB0\fR\-\fB7\fR
Selects a tape unit digit, used to build a default archive device
name of the form
.B /dev/mt\fIn\fR
(or
.B /dev/rmt\fIn\fR
if the blocking factor is 1) when neither
.B f
nor an
.I archive
operand is given.
.IP \fBb\fR
Takes the next argument as the
.I blocking
factor, the number of archive records per physical record (default 1,
maximum 20).
.IP \fBf\fR
Takes the next argument as the
.I archive
pathname instead of using a tape device name.
An
.I archive
of
.B \-
means the standard input (for
.BR t / x )
or the standard output (for
.BR c ).
.IP \fBl\fR
Print a message for each link
.B tar
was unable to resolve while writing an archive.
.IP \fBm\fR
When extracting, leave the modification time of each restored file as
of the time it was extracted, instead of restoring the time recorded
in the archive.
.IP \fBv\fR
Verbose; print the name of each
.I file
as it is processed.
.IP \fBw\fR
Ask, for each
.IR file ,
whether to add it to or extract it from the archive.
.IP \fBz\fR
Pipe the archive through
.B compress
while writing it, or through
.B uncompress
while reading it.
.B z
may not be combined with
.B r
or
.BR u .
.IP \fBU\fR
Stop reading the archive as soon as an empty header block is seen,
for archives written by another
.B tar
that pads with a run of zero blocks instead of a single empty header.
Without
.BR U ,
.B tar
skips over an isolated empty header block looking for further
entries.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B compress
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B tar
complains and skips an entry whose checksum does not match, and
reports any file it could not create, open, or link.
It exits with the current value of
.BR errno ,
zero if no system call has failed.
.SH NOTES
The tape unit digits, blocking factor, and default device names apply
mainly when
.I archive
is a magnetic tape; on a fixed disk or floppy, name the archive
explicitly with
.BR f .
